finish('vp-versus-vnp-permanent-arithmetic-circuits',
 id='TCS-0005',title='Does the permanent have polynomial-size arithmetic circuits?',
 area='Algebraic and numerical computation',criterion='models',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Over the field $\mathbb C$, do there exist constants C,k such that, for every integer n≥1, the polynomial $\operatorname{perm}_n(X)=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\prod_{i=1}^n x_{i,\sigma(i)}$ can be computed by an arithmetic circuit with at most C(n+1)^k gates? Equivalently, is $\mathrm{VP}_{\mathbb C}=\mathrm{VNP}_{\mathbb C}$? The usual conjecture is that the answer is no.''',
 definitions=r'''X has n² independent indeterminates and S_n is the set of permutations of {1,…,n}. A circuit is a finite directed acyclic graph with input nodes labeled by variables or arbitrary complex constants, binary +, −, × operation gates, and one output. Its size counts operation gates; intermediate results can feed any number of later gates. Division, comparisons, oracle gates, approximation, and limits of circuits are absent. The output must equal the permanent as a formal polynomial. Circuits may depend on n without a uniform construction algorithm; constants have unit arithmetic cost, with no bit-encoding restriction. VP consists of polynomial families with polynomially bounded numbers of variables and total degrees, computed by polynomial-size circuits. VNP consists of polynomial families f_n(x)=Σ_{y∈{0,1}^{p(n)}} g_n(x,y), for some polynomial p and some such polynomial-size, polynomial-degree circuit family g. The witness sum is over Boolean strings even though the coefficients lie in C.''',
 answer_criterion='Construct the stated circuit family, or prove that the minimum circuit size of the permanent is not bounded by any polynomial in n. A lower bound for every polynomial-size general circuit family suffices. Lower bounds only for formulas, monotone circuits, bounded-depth circuits, or determinantal representations do not by themselves settle this formulation.',
 context=[
  paragraph('The permanent sums one product for every perfect matching in a complete bipartite graph. Substituting a zero-one adjacency matrix counts perfect matchings. This card asks for an exact symbolic arithmetic computation valid on all complex matrices. The model permits cancellations and unrestricted reuse of intermediate results, so it captures substantially more than enumerating matchings.'),
  paragraph('The permanent and determinant have the same permutation products, but the determinant attaches a sign to each one. Determinants have polynomial-size division-free circuits despite their factorially many displayed terms. Thus counting the monomials in the permanent is not a circuit lower bound: the question is whether an equally effective algebraic compression exists for its unsigned sum.', 'book'),
  paragraph('Valiant makes this example universal. Every family in VNP reduces to a polynomially larger permanent by substitutions of variables and constants. A polynomial circuit for the permanent would therefore compress every efficient Boolean-witness sum in the definition of VNP. Conversely, separating these classes forces the permanent to escape polynomial circuit size.', 'valiant'),
  paragraph('Important partial results restrict how computations are organized. A formula cannot reuse an intermediate result; a constant-depth circuit permits only a fixed number of operation layers. Even proving that the permanent has no small determinant representation would rule out only that representation model. None of these restrictions is part of the circuit model in this card.', 'book'),
  paragraph('Recent lower bounds illustrate both the progress and the gap. Superpolynomial bounds are known at every fixed depth, and new work constructs polynomial maps that evade low-complexity parametrizations. The latter can yield stronger circuit lower bounds if its degree and dimension parameters improve sufficiently. The constructions currently cited do not give the required unrestricted superpolynomial bound.', 'recent')],
 why='This is the central separation question for arithmetic computation. It tests whether summing exponentially many efficiently describable algebraic contributions inherently requires more computation than evaluating one polynomial-size circuit, and organizes lower-bound research across algebra, geometry, and pseudorandomness.',
 importance=dict(score=96,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='A foundational separation for general arithmetic circuits, with a complete concrete target in the permanent and consequences across algebraic complexity and explicit lower-bound constructions.'),
 progress=[
  step('1963','Ryser’s inclusion-exclusion expression gives an exponential-size formula, for example O(n²2^n) arithmetic operations. This substantially improves direct enumeration of n! permutations but remains far above a polynomial bound.','book'),
  step('1979','Valiant establishes algebraic completeness of the permanent over fields of characteristic different from two. Over C this yields the equivalence between polynomial-size permanent circuits and VP=VNP.','valiant'),
  step('2021','Limaye, Srinivasan, and Tavenas prove superpolynomial lower bounds against arithmetic circuits of every constant depth in characteristic zero. Those circuits may have unbounded fan-in, with size accounting for their description; the fixed depth is an essential restriction. The result does not separate general VP and VNP.','depth'),
  step('2026-08','Narayanan relates elusive polynomial maps to expansion of iterated sumsets and obtains improved superlinear bounds in certain sublogarithmic-depth regimes. An explicit elusive-curve construction has exponential degree; the paper explains that its parameters do not yet imply VP≠VNP.','recent')],
 references=[
  ref('book','Mathematics and Computation','Avi Wigderson',2018,'https://www.math.ias.edu/files/mathandcomp.pdf','March 2018 draft, Sections 12.3–12.4; Conjectures 12.17 and 12.21; Theorem 12.22'),
  ref('valiant','Completeness Classes in Algebra','Leslie G. Valiant',1979,'https://doi.org/10.1145/800135.804419','STOC 1979, pp. 249–261; historical foundation of algebraic completeness; modern circuit-class formulation in the cited book'),
  ref('depth','Superpolynomial Lower Bounds Against Low-Depth Algebraic Circuits','Nutan Limaye; Srikanth Srinivasan; Sébastien Tavenas',2021,'https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2021/081/','Revision 1, 6 July 2021; FOCS 2021, held February 2022; DOI 10.1109/FOCS52979.2021.00083'),
  ref('recent','Arithmetic circuit lower bounds from sumset expansion','Anand Kumar Narayanan',2026,'https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/138/','Submitted 8 July, published 9 August 2026; Sections 1.1–1.4 and Theorems 1–3')],
 status='source_open',status_note='The August 2026 primary paper still identifies the unrestricted VP-versus-VNP separation as open and explicitly limits its new constructions. No general resolution was located in the status search through 10 September 2026.',
 formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
